<article class="env-lemma" id="082X">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/082X">Lemma <span data-tag="082X">67.16.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. There exists a closed subspace $Z \subset Y$ such that $f$ factors through $Z$ and such that for any other closed subspace $Z' \subset Y$ such that $f$ factors through $Z'$ we have $Z \subset Z'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\mathcal{I} = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(\mathcal{O}_ Y \to f_*\mathcal{O}_ X)$. If $\mathcal{I}$ is quasi-coherent then we just take $Z$ to be the closed subscheme determined by $\mathcal{I}$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/03MB" data-tag="03MB">67.13.1</a>. In general the lemma requires us to show that there exists a largest quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals $\mathcal{I}'$ contained in $\mathcal{I}$. This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/04CK" data-tag="04CK">67.14.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>